ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE ACT ON THE MANAGEMENT AND UTILIZATION OF MARINE HEALING RESOURCES

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on the Management and Utilization of Marine Healing Resources and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Amendment of Minor Matters in Basic Plans)
"Where minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree are amended" in the proviso of Article 5 (4) of the Act on the Management and Utilization of Marine Healing Resources (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means any of the following cases:
1. Where content is amended due to amendments to other statutes or regulations or plans under such statutes or regulations, within the scope of the objectives and implementation directions of policies for the management and utilization of marine healing resources under Article 5 (2) 1 of the Act;
2. Where a simple mistake, clerical error, omission, or comparable manifest error is corrected.

Article 3 (Formulation and Amendment of Regional Plans)
(1) Matters to be included in a regional plan for the management and utilization of marine healing resources under Article 6 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "regional plan") shall be prescribed by ordinance of the Special Metropolitan City, a Metropolitan City, a Special Self-Governing City, a Do, or a Special Self-Governing Province (hereinafter referred to as a "City/Do").
(2) Upon formulating or amending a regional plan, the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as a "Mayor/Do Governor") shall publicly announce its content on the website of the relevant City/Do and make it available for public inspection for at least 14 days.
(3) Upon formulating or amending a regional plan, a Mayor/Do Governor shall notify the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries of its content without delay.

Article 4 (Evaluation of Regional Plan Implementation Performance)
(1) A Mayor/Do Governor who has formulated a regional plan pursuant to Article 6 (1) of the Act shall submit the implementation performance of the regional plan for the previous year to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries by January 31 of each year.
(2) The implementation performance under paragraph (1) shall include the following matters:
1. Utilization of marine healing resources and marine healing services;
2. Development and operation of marine healing facilities;
3. Support provided to residents of coastal areas and fishing villages in connection with marine healing;
4. Training and utilization of specialized personnel under Article 27 of the Act;
5. Other matters concerning the progress of projects for the management and utilization of marine healing resources.
(3) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall evaluate the implementation performance submitted pursuant to paragraph (1) and notify the Mayor/Do Governor of the results.
(4) Where necessary for evaluating implementation performance pursuant to paragraph (3), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may request relevant experts, institutions, organizations, or others to conduct investigations, analyses, or similar work. In such cases, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may pay the expenses necessary for such investigations, analyses, or similar work within budgetary limits.
(5) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall determine and publicly notify the criteria and methods for evaluating regional plan implementation performance and other matters, in addition to those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4).

Article 5 (Entrustment of Duties to Investigate Marine Healing Resources)
"Institutions or organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 7 (3) of the Act means the following institutions or organizations:
1. A Marine Healing Management Organization under Article 22 (1) of the Act;
2. Institutions or organizations determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries from among public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions or nonprofit corporations established with permission from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries pursuant to Article 32 of the Civil Act.

Article 6 (Institutions for Establishing and Operating the Marine Healing Resources Information System)
"Institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 8 (3) of the Act means the following institutions:
1. A Marine Healing Management Organization under Article 22 (1) of the Act;
2. Institutions determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries from among public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions or nonprofit corporations established with permission from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries pursuant to Article 32 of the Civil Act.

Article 7 (Matters to Consider in Designating Marine Healing Districts)
"Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 9 (2) 5 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Formulation of plans for operation in conjunction with medical, welfare, educational, and other facilities;
2. Formulation of measures to prevent marine pollution and manage the environment;
3. Preparation of measures to improve access to a marine healing district;
4. Gathering opinions from local residents;
5. Other matters deemed necessary and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 8 (Requests for Submission of Materials and Gathering of Opinions)
(1) Where necessary for designating or amending a marine healing district pursuant to Article 9 of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may request the competent Mayor/Do Governor, the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu (the head of a Gu means the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply), or a project implementer under Article 13 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "project implementer") to submit relevant materials.
(2) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends to gather opinions from local residents pursuant to Article 9 (4) and (5) of the Act, the Minister shall publicly announce the main content of the designation or amendment of the marine healing district in at least two daily newspapers whose principal distribution area is the relevant area, in the Official Gazette, on the website of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, or by similar means, and shall forward it to the heads of the relevant Sis/Guns/Gus for public inspection for at least 14 days.
(3) Any person who has an opinion on the content publicly announced pursuant to paragraph (2) may submit the opinion to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries or the head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu within five days from the end of the inspection period.
(4) The head of the competent Si/Gun/Gu shall forward opinions submitted pursuant to paragraph (3) to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.
(5) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall examine whether to reflect the opinions submitted pursuant to paragraph (3) and notify each person who submitted an opinion of the results within 30 days from the end of the inspection period.

Article 9 (Amendment of Minor Matters in Marine Healing Districts)
"Where minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree are amended" in the proviso of Article 9 (5) of the Act means any of the following cases:
1. Where the area of a marine healing district is changed within a range of 10 percent;
2. Where the name of a marine healing district is changed due to a change in an administrative district or similar circumstances;
3. Where a simple mistake, clerical error, omission, or comparable manifest error is corrected.

Article 10 (Public Notice of Designation or Amendment of Marine Healing Districts)
"Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 11 (1) 5 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Statutes and regulations forming the basis for designating or amending the marine healing district;
2. Reasons for and purposes of designating or amending the marine healing district;
3. The date of designation or amendment of the marine healing district;
4. Matters concerning inspection of materials related to designation or amendment of the marine healing district;
5. The status of ownership, fishery rights, and other rights in the marine healing district and surrounding areas;
6. Topographical drawings and the like under Article 8 (3) of the Framework Act on the Regulation of Land Use (including nautical charts).

Article 11 (Revocation of Designation of Marine Healing Districts)
When revoking the designation of a marine healing district pursuant to Article 12 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall publicly notify the following matters in the Official Gazette:
1. Statutes and regulations forming the basis for revoking the designation of the marine healing district;
2. Reasons for revoking the designation of the marine healing district;
3. The date of designation of the marine healing district and the effective date of revocation of its designation;
4. Matters concerning the restoration of land use plans and the like under Article 12 (3) of the Act to their former state;
5. Matters concerning cancellation of deemed authorizations, permissions, and the like under Article 12 (3) of the Act;
6. Topographical drawings and the like under Article 8 (3) of the Framework Act on the Regulation of Land Use (including nautical charts).

Article 12 (Designation of Project Implementers)
① "Public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 13 (1) 2 of the Act means the following institutions:
1. The Korea Fisheries Infrastructure Public Agency under Article 57 (1) of the Fishing Villages and Fishery Harbors Act;
2. The Korea Rural Community Corporation under Article 3 of the Korea Rural Community Corporation and Farmland Management Fund Act;
3. Other public institutions that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries recognizes as capable of implementing marine healing district development projects.
② "Private investors meeting qualification requirements prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as capital requirements" in Article 13 (1) 4 of the Act means the following persons:
1. A person registered in a business category for performing general construction works under the Framework Act on the Construction Industry (limited to civil engineering works, building construction works, and civil engineering and building construction works in attached Table 2 of the Enforcement Decree of that Act), whose assessed construction capacity amount publicly announced under Article 23 of that Act is at least the average annual project cost required for the relevant development project (excluding compensation costs);
2. An entrusted-management real estate investment company under subparagraph 1 (b) of Article 2 of the Real Estate Investment Company Act;
3. A public-private joint corporation under subparagraph 13 of Article 2 of the Act on Public-Private Partnerships in Infrastructure;
4. A trust business entity under the Financial Investment Services and Capital Markets Act that is subject to external audit under Article 4 of the Act on External Audit of Stock Companies, etc.;
5. A person who owns at least 50 percent of the land area within the project zone;
6. A real estate trust company entrusted with land accounting for at least one third of the land area within the project zone.

Article 13 (Changes to Minor Matters in Marine Healing District Development Plans)
① "Cases where a change to minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree is intended" in the proviso to Article 14 (1) of the Act means the following cases:
1. Where a change to the project implementer's name (meaning a change of name by the same person) or address is intended (where the project implementer is a corporation, meaning a change to the corporation's name, its representative's name, or the corporation's address);
2. Where a correction to the size of the project implementation area due to a surveying error, etc. is intended, without changing the area in which the project is implemented;
3. Where a change to the content is intended due to changes in other statutes or regulations or in plans under them, within the scope of the direction and objectives for the development of the marine healing district under Article 14 (2) 4 of the Act;
4. Where the correction of a simple mistake, clerical error, omission, or comparable obvious error is intended.
② Where a matter under any subparagraph of paragraph ① in the marine healing district development plan under the main clause of Article 14 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "development plan") has changed, the project implementer shall notify the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries of the details without delay.

Article 14 (Content of Development Plans) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 14 (2) 7 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Matters concerning demand for projects within the marine healing district;
2. Matters concerning the installation of infrastructure, such as roads, within the marine healing district;
3. Matters concerning the installation and operation of marine healing facilities;
4. Matters concerning environmental conservation in the marine healing district and surrounding areas;
5. Other matters necessary for the development of and support for the marine healing district.

Article 15 (Feasibility Studies of Development Plans)
① Where a project implementer intends to apply for a study of the economic and social feasibility of a development plan under Article 15 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "feasibility study"), the project implementer shall submit an application for a feasibility study prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, together with the documents prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, to an institution falling under any subparagraph of paragraph ②.
② "Specialized institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 15 (1) of the Act means the following institutions:
1. The Marine Healing Management Organization under Article 22 (1) of the Act (excluding cases where the Marine Healing Management Organization is the relevant project implementer);
2. Other corporations that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries recognizes as capable of conducting feasibility studies and publicly notifies as such.
③ Detailed criteria for feasibility studies shall be as specified in attached Table 1.
④ An institution that receives an application for a feasibility study under paragraph ① shall calculate the fee in accordance with the criteria determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, taking into account the personnel, equipment, study period, etc. required for the feasibility study, and shall notify the project implementer that applied for the feasibility study of the fee.
⑤ A project implementer that receives notification under paragraph ④ shall pay the fee in cash or by such means as electronic money or electronic payment through an information and communications network.
⑥ In addition to the matters prescribed in paragraphs ④ and ⑤, matters necessary for the notification, payment, and refund of fees shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 16 (Changes to Minor Matters in Implementation Plans)
① "Cases where a change to minor matters prescribed by Presidential Decree is intended" in the proviso to the introductory provisions of Article 17 (1) of the Act means the following cases:
1. Where a change to the schedule of the annual project implementation plan is intended without changing the overall project implementation period;
2. Where a change to the content is intended due to changes in other statutes or regulations or in plans under them, to an extent that does not change the basic direction of the implementation plan under Article 17 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "implementation plan");
3. Where the correction of a simple mistake, clerical error, omission, or comparable obvious error is intended.
② Where a matter under any subparagraph of paragraph ① in an implementation plan has changed, the project implementer shall notify the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries of the details of the change without delay.

Article 17 (Matters to Be Reviewed in Implementation Plans) "Whether the plan is appropriate with respect to matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 17 (2) 3 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Whether the plan for securing and installing facilities for preventing marine pollution is appropriate;
2. Whether the plan for purchasing and compensating for land, objects, rights, etc. at the project implementation site is appropriate;
3. Whether the plan is appropriate with respect to other matters that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems necessary for approval of the implementation plan.

Article 18 (Public Notice of Approval of Implementation Plans) A public notice under the former part of Article 17 (3) of the Act shall include the following matters:
1. The year, month, and day of the public notice;
2. The name, purpose, and outline of the project;
3. The project implementer's name (in the case of a corporation, meaning the corporation's name and its representative's name);
4. The location and area of the project implementation site;
5. The project implementation period (including the scheduled dates of commencement and completion of construction);
6. Topographical drawings, etc. under Article 8 (3) of the Framework Act on the Regulation of Land Use (including nautical charts).

Article 19 (Designation of the Marine Healing Management Organization) "Institutions or organizations prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 22 (1) of the Act means the following institutions or organizations:
1. Public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
2. Nonprofit corporations established with permission from the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries under Article 32 of the Civil Act;
3. Government-funded research institutes under Article 8 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes, etc.

Article 20 (Criteria, etc. for Establishment and Designation of the Marine Healing Management Organization)
(1) The criteria for establishing and designating the Marine Healing Management Organization under Article 22(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Marine Healing Management Organization") shall be as follows:
1. It shall have expertise in the marine healing industry or related policy fields;
2. It shall have the organizational structure and personnel necessary for surveys and research on marine healing resources and the promotion of business development;
3. It shall have the facilities and equipment necessary to establish and operate the information system for marine healing resources under Article 8(1) of the Act.
(2) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries designates the Marine Healing Management Organization pursuant to Article 22(1) of the Act, the Minister shall issue a certificate of designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(3) The head of the Marine Healing Management Organization shall submit the previous year's business implementation results and the business plan for the current year to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries by January 31 each year, and, where the business plan is changed, shall notify the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries of the details without delay.
(4) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (3), detailed matters necessary for the establishment, designation, and operation of the Marine Healing Management Organization shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 21 (Projects to Promote the Marine Healing Industry) "Projects prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 5 of Article 26 of the Act means the following projects:
1. Development of programs for regional specialization of the marine healing industry;
2. Development and dissemination of marine healing services;
3. International cooperation concerning the marine healing industry.

Article 21-2 (Support for Business Startups, etc.)
(1) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries intends, pursuant to Article 26-2(1) of the Act, to provide the support specified in the subparagraphs of that paragraph to persons who intend to start businesses or commercialize technologies related to marine healing, the Minister shall publicly announce on the website of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries a support plan that includes the following matters:
1. The details of the support;
2. The method and period for applying for support;
3. The procedures and methods for selecting recipients of support;
4. Other matters necessary for the support.
(2) A person who intends to start a business or commercialize technology related to marine healing and seeks support pursuant to Article 26-2(1) of the Act shall apply to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries in accordance with the support plan publicly announced pursuant to paragraph (1).
(3) When selecting recipients of support upon receiving applications under paragraph (2), the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall consider the feasibility of the relevant business startup or technology commercialization related to marine healing, its commercial potential, including market demand, the extent to which it may contribute to promoting the marine healing industry, and other relevant factors.

Article 22 (Designation, etc. of Specialized Personnel Training Institutions)
(1) "Institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 27(2) of the Act means the following institutions:
1. Schools under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act that have established and operate curricula related to marine healing;
2. Public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
3. Research institutes, institutions, or organizations that conduct research activities, etc. on marine healing.
(2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may designate an institution that meets all the designation criteria in attached Table 2 as a specialized personnel training institution under Article 27(2) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "specialized personnel training institution").
(3) A person who seeks designation as a specialized personnel training institution shall submit an application for designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, accompanied by the documents prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, to the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu.
(4) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu designates a specialized personnel training institution, the Minister, Mayor/Do Governor, or head of a Si/Gun/Gu shall issue a certificate of designation prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(5) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may provide support to specialized personnel training institutions for the following expenses pursuant to Article 27(3) of the Act:
1. Instructors' lecture fees;
2. The costs of producing textbooks and purchasing equipment and materials for practical training;
3. Other expenses deemed necessary for education and training by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.
(6) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) through (5), matters necessary for the designation and operation of specialized personnel training institutions shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries.

Article 23 (Revocation of Designation of Specialized Personnel Training Institutions) Pursuant to Article 27(5) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries, a Mayor/Do Governor, or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu may revoke the designation of a specialized personnel training institution where it falls under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Where it no longer meets the designation criteria under Article 22(2);
2. Where it obtained designation by falsehood or improper means;
3. Where it has not trained specialized personnel for a continuous period of at least one year without justifiable grounds;
4. Where it used the financial support received for other purposes.

Article 24 (Measures for Providing Marine Healing Services)
(1) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may implement the following measures to provide marine healing services to the public pursuant to Article 28(1) of the Act:
1. Support for the use of marine healing services by economically and socially vulnerable groups;
2. Development and dissemination of business models for marine healing services;
3. Collection, provision, and publicity of information on marine healing services;
4. Support for the standardization and dissemination of marine healing services.
(2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may implement the following measures to provide marine healing services to residents of coastal areas and fishing villages pursuant to Article 28(2) of the Act:
1. Development and utilization of local marine healing resources and their preferential provision to local residents;
2. Establishment of marine healing facilities for local residents.

Article 25 (Compilation and Dissemination of Statistics)
(1) Statistics on the status of marine healing resources and marine healing facilities, the actual state of marine healing services, etc. under Article 29 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "marine healing statistics") shall include the following matters:
1. The status of the management and utilization of marine healing resources;
2. The status of the establishment and operation of marine healing facilities;
3. The status of the development and dissemination of marine healing services;
4. The status of research and development related to the marine healing industry;
5. The status of the training and utilization of specialized personnel under Article 27 of the Act;
6. Other matters for which the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems it necessary to compile statistics.
(2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall compile and manage marine healing statistics on an annual basis.
(3) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall make marine healing statistics public in a manner that allows their wide dissemination, such as by posting them on the website of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries.
(4) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall ensure that marine healing statistics are linked to the marine healing resources information system under Article 8 (1) of the Act for the systematic collection and management of marine healing statistics.

Article 26 (Support Projects for Residents of Coastal Areas and Fishing Villages)
(1) A person who develops and operates marine healing facilities within a marine healing district (hereinafter referred to as a "marine healing facility business operator") may implement the following support projects pursuant to Article 30 (1) of the Act:
1. Establishment of marine healing education and experience facilities for residents of the coastal area or fishing village in which the relevant facilities are located, or financial support for such establishment;
2. Financial support for environmental improvements, such as the installation of wastewater or human waste treatment facilities in the marine healing district and its adjacent areas;
3. Other projects that the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries deems necessary to improve the living environment and promote the welfare of local residents, etc.
(2) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over an area designated as a marine healing district may, to support the efficient implementation of a support project under paragraph (1), request that a marine healing facility business operator intending to implement such a project formulate and submit a support project plan that includes the following matters:
1. The purpose and outline of the project;
2. The target area, details, and scope of the support project;
3. An implementation plan for each phase and measures to secure financial resources;
4. Other matters necessary to carry out the support project.
(3) When formulating a support project plan under paragraph (2), a marine healing facility business operator may hear the opinions of local residents eligible for support and reflect the results of such consultation in the plan.

Article 27 (Preferential Employment of Local Residents, Etc.)
(1) Where a marine healing facility business operator intends, pursuant to Article 30 (2) of the Act, to give employment preference to persons relocated due to project implementation and residents of the area in which the marine healing facilities are located, the head of a Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over the area designated as a marine healing district may request that the marine healing facility business operator prepare and submit a preferential employment plan in advance.
(2) The head of a Si/Gun/Gu who has received a preferential employment plan pursuant to paragraph (1) may take necessary measures, such as providing employment information, to ensure that persons relocated due to project implementation and local residents within the jurisdiction can be given employment preference.

Article 28 (Delegation of Authority) Pursuant to Article 33 (1) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall delegate to the head of a regional maritime affairs and fisheries office the authority under Article 17 of the Act to approve implementation plans and amendments thereto and to publicly notify and transmit implementation plans.

Article 29 (Entrustment of Functions)
(1) Pursuant to Article 33 (2) of the Act, the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries may entrust the following functions to an institution designated under paragraph (2):
1. Joint research and development and quality management of marine healing resources and marine healing services under Article 19 (1) 2 of the Act;
2. Development and dissemination of marine healing programs under Article 24 of the Act;
3. Compilation and dissemination of statistics under Article 29 of the Act.
(2) The Minister of Oceans and Fisheries shall designate the entity to be entrusted with the functions under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) from among the following institutions:
1. The Marine Healing Management Organization under Article 22 (1) of the Act;
2. A government-funded science and technology research institute under Article 8 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Science and Technology Research Institutes;
3. The Korea Fisheries Infrastructure Public Agency under Article 57 of the Fishing Villages and Fishery Harbors Act;
4. A government-funded research institute under Article 8 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutes;
5. The Korea Tourism Organization under the Korea Tourism Organization Act;
6. The Korea Marine Environment Management Corporation under Article 96 of the Marine Environment Management Act.
(3) Where the Minister of Oceans and Fisheries entrusts functions pursuant to paragraphs (1) and (2), the Minister shall publicly announce the entrusted institution, the details of the entrusted functions, and other necessary matters in the Official Gazette, on the website of the Ministry of Oceans and Fisheries, or by other means.

Article 30 (Criteria for Imposition of Administrative Fines) The criteria for imposing administrative fines under Article 37 (1) through (3) of the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 3.

